WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:04.810
We have a voicemail number which is 2 1 9
2.

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:06.359
David P..

00:00:06.359 --> 00:00:11.780
Here's a question that many in the audience
have been asking, while others in the audience

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:13.299
reject the framing.

00:00:13.299 --> 00:00:14.990
Let's take a listen to the question.

00:00:14.990 --> 00:00:16.789
Hey, David.

00:00:16.789 --> 00:00:20.199
Yvonne from Corona, California.

00:00:20.199 --> 00:00:25.980
Just wondering one paragraph, look, losing
so much since I'm getting sick of it.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:30.619
I mean, this whole healthy AOC business and,
you know, people getting mad about her actually

00:00:30.619 --> 00:00:32.009
trying to be a politician.

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:36.309
And, you know, people saying they awful for
Biden.

00:00:36.309 --> 00:00:37.309
Yeah.

00:00:37.309 --> 00:00:38.309
I'm sick of it.

00:00:38.309 --> 00:00:40.969
And uncertainty progressives just like losing
anyways.

00:00:40.969 --> 00:00:43.489
So, yeah, I mean, this is a fair question.

00:00:43.489 --> 00:00:46.969
Not everybody agrees with the framing that
it's not cleared.

00:00:46.969 --> 00:00:49.350
Not not all progressives like losing.

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:50.930
I think there's a couple of things going on.

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:57.850
There are progressives who have taken the
position that short term losses serve some

00:00:57.850 --> 00:00:59.360
bigger purpose.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:02.660
That will be a future win of some kind.

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:06.450
That's a position that is not an irrational
position.

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:09.700
I just don't think it's practical and I don't
think it actually works.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.380
But you can rationally come to the conclusion
that you're willing to tolerate short term

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:17.860
and even medium term losses for some sort
of longer term win.

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:26.000
There are also people who genuinely don't
see what you and I might see as a loss.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:27.140
As a loss, right.

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:28.710
Like there's disagreement there.

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:30.990
The AOC thing is a perfect example.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:36.170
ICAO sees move to pragmatism as a positive
thing.

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:40.950
That suggests she has better political instincts
than I may be previously thought.

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:46.660
I got crushed for that position yesterday
on the program and a ton of people wrote to

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:49.330
me saying this is simply not a win.

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:53.450
In other words, it's not incrementalism versus
revolution.

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:57.320
It's simply disagreeing that this is going
to lead to any kind of win.

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:00.220
So I don't believe progressives love losing.

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:08.970
However, there is a fraction of progressives
who prefer to be in the underdog victim martyr,

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:13.030
always trying to claw from behind position.

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:16.410
And that is very motivating to a lot of people.

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:17.890
It's a good business strategy.

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:24.590
I know that there's a lot of, you know, sort
of activism outfits that essentially are constantly

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:30.420
fundraising on the basis of this come from
behind martyr victim position.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:32.110
So I think that there's a little bit of everything.

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:33.780
There's there's a sort of all of the above.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:36.590
I don't believe most progressives like losing.

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:41.590
They just have a disagreement about what we
what it means to win or lose at a fundamental

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:42.590
level.

